<article xml:id="Chapter-2" label="Chapter-2">
  <title>Chapter 2 Submission</title>

  <section>
    <title>2.1.3 Proof</title>

    <figure>
      <image source="asset-2-1-3-submission.png">
<shortdescription>Image required for the proof of Theorem 2-1-3.</shortdescription>
</image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Take the figure above. Because angles <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\gamma</m> form
      a linear pair, the supplement postulate indicates that
      <m>m(\angle\alpha)+m(\angle\gamma)=180</m> <degree/>. We also know that
      <m>\gamma</m> and <m>\beta</m> form a linear pair, so we know that
      <m>m(\angle\gamma)+m(\angle\beta)=180</m> <degree/>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus,
      <m>m(\angle\alpha)+m(\angle\gamma)=m(\angle\gamma)+m(\angle\beta)</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      So, <m>m(\angle\alpha)=m(\angle\beta)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus, <m>\angle\alpha\cong\angle\beta</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We can show by similar processes that
      <m>\angle\gamma\cong\angle\sigma</m>, so the theorem is proven.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>2.1.17 Proof</title>

    <figure>
      <image source="asset-2-1-17-image.png">
<shortdescription>Image required for the proof of Theorem 2-1-17.</shortdescription>
</image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Take the above figure and suppose that line segments <m>i</m> and <m>j</m>
      to be congruent and the angles <m>\angle CBA</m> AND <m>\angle DBA</m> to
      be congruent. Note that line segment <m>f</m> is congruent to itself.
      Then, per the Isosceles Triangle Theorem,
      <m>\angle ACB\cong\angle ADB</m>. Thus, by our congruences, we know that
      <m>\triangle ABC\cong\triangle ABD</m> per Angle Angle Side congruence.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>
